    "title": "File:Frilled Lizard (2863780749).jpg",
    "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Frilled_Lizard_%282863780749%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frilled_Lizard_(2863780749).jpg",
    "original_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Frilled_Lizard_%282863780749%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
    "license": "CC BY 2.0",
    "artist": "<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/28567825@N03\">Cliff</a> from Arlington, Virginia, USA",
    "description": "This lizard is famous for its spectacular neck frill, displayed when the animal is disturbed or alarmed. Most predators are frightened off by this display of aggression. At the same time that the lizard raises its frill or ruff – almost like an umbrella, it opens its mouth wide, darkens its color, and stands rocking on its hind legs, all the while making an ominous hissing sound. The coloring of this lizard, generally agreed to be Australia’s most spectacular, varies with location. Those in the Northern Territory and Western Australia tend to be brick red with yellow and orange on their frills. Those belonging to the more eastern species are more uniformly gray to brown. The mouth lining is light yellow, and the animal’s legs and tail are long."
